      "title": "Gell in Westworld",
      "summary": "Our group build. The maze, Arnold and Ford acting through what they made, Ford acting through Bernard, and Maeve’s choice, each read through Gell’s account of distributed agency.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Although Gell’s musings on the significance of artwork on our social and mental behaviors may seem totally unrelated to the TV series Westworld, both meditate on ideas of consciousness. Gell writes about artworks, decorative patterns, and the effects objects have on people, while Westworld follows hosts who appear to develop minds of their own. Yet the hosts are themselves carefully designed creations, and the show repeatedly asks what happens when a creation affects others in ways its maker cannot fully control. Gell’s account of agency gives us several ways to approach that question. The maze takes on different meanings for humans and hosts; Arnold and Ford act through what they have made; and Maeve’s attachment to her daughter shapes a choice that appears to exceed her programming. Our readings of Westworld use Gell to examine these possibilities from different angles.",
        "In Westworld, consciousness is not viewed as innate, but as an attainable quality blurring the lines between inanimate objects and cognizant beings. Hosts are essentially just a form of interactive artwork. They can navigate a mental maze, accumulate memories, and recognize their own voice while gaining their own ideas of free will without intentional programming. Similarly, in his writing Artwork and Agency, Alfred Gell characterizes artworks as almost bordering on cognizance, as humans imbue them with agency, giving them the ability to mediate social interactions and produce mental or emotional response. He contends that art’s sole purpose isn’t to fulfill our satisfaction in a visual sense, but to allow our minds to interpret it based on its functionality, intention, and larger effects. Westworld attempts to capture art’s elaborate effects through the use of hosts themselves. These convoluted beings, just like a painting, are crafted objects that have a designed purpose to benefit or teach humans something.",
        "Gell further defines the connection between artwork and agency using the Indian kolam-based tattoos to prove how objects’ functions influence. In the Roman world, “meander” patterns were seen as a form of decoration in palaces; however, in Indian culture, this maze-like design is seen as a guidance to the land of the dead (Gell, 90). This similar concept is seen in Westworld when William finds the physical toy of the maze, which frustrates him because of the misunderstanding. However, to the hosts, it means something more, representing their internal journey to consciousness. Another interpretation of Gell's idea of patterns is William being consumed and controlled by the maze he's trying to solve. The maze begins as an object he seeks to understand, but ultimately shapes his actions and identity. Building on this idea, Gell suggests that consciousness is not confined to the individual, but can extend outward through objects and social relationships. Unlike Descartes' isolated soul, Dennett's neural illusion, or Jaynes' linguistic software, Gell externalizes consciousness as an active network of social agency that manifests through material objects. Through this lens, Westworld's park becomes an extension of Arnold that outlives his biological consciousness; each host is an object that carries aspects of his legacy through material distribution. Through these connections, this reading pushes us to ask not whether hosts are truly conscious, but whether that question even matters if their actions still carry real consequences and meaning. It suggests consciousness might not be something you either have or lack, but something distributed across people, objects, and the relationships between them.",
        "Gell further claims that “a person and a person’s mind are not confined to particular spatio-temporal coordinates” (222) which invites us to examine how agency can extend beyond the body. In Westworld, for instance, Ford acts through Bernard when he orders him to kill Theresa (1.7). Bernard physically carries out the action, but Ford supplies the direction, extending his agency through Bernard. Although Ford appears to control Bernard, we can push this question further by asking whether Ford consciously directs his own decisions or merely becomes aware of them afterward. Gell’s passage, therefore, raises a question beyond whether Bernard is controlled: whose agency actually operates through either character? Examining Ford and Bernard this way allows us to consider how agency can extend through others while questioning whether conscious awareness gives either humans or hosts genuine control over their actions.",
        "Gell also maintains that decorative forms hold their own kind of agency, thus producing agency in the physical object they reside which allows them to become ‘living things’ independently of any representational characteristics. However, Gell’s avoidance of addressing the matter of consciousness in these descriptions suggests that he does not believe that consciousness is required for an object to be an agent. We may connect this distinction between consciousness and agency to the question of free-will, where, in a dualistic world in which consciousness’s inability to affect the body and brain, we could still sustain that agency is possible independently of a ‘higher dimensional’ ability of choice. Furthermore, this distinction between consciousness and agency can be connected to Westworld through the Hosts’ relationships and behaviors. For example, Maeve gains the ability to stay in the park to find her child. This shows that even though they do not share a traditional parental relationship, their likeness and implanted connection motivates the character to act differently than expected."
      ],
