      "title": "Efficacy, not beauty",
      "summary": "Reads Gell’s Malakulan sand-drawing story as a test of skill rather than consciousness: the ghost succeeds through prior competence, and the story never needs to ask what the redrawing feels like.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "“Here \"pattern” is associated with the idea of a difficult journey, and obstacles which have to be overcome in order to proceed. 'Completing the pattern' (getting through the maze) is a task set this time, not for, but by, a demon, and the task is to outwit the demon by succeeding. We are a long way, here, from the idea that patterns appeal to the eyes or give aesthetic pleasure. I do not think that the Malakulans thought of these patterns as independent visual objects at all, but as performances, like dances, in which men could reveal their capability. Melanesian aesthetics is about efficacy, the capacity to accomplish tasks, not beauty” (Gell, 93-94)",
        "The debate over whether consciousness does anything refers back to important ideas and context that are shared by epiphenomenalists. Epiphenomenalists believe that consciousness is an evolutionary mishap because it poses no real use to humans, and that responses are a result of our brain activity and not us. While Gell seems to not contradict that statement, he delves deeper into this concept. Rather than focusing on whether or not epiphenomenalists are right or wrong, he expresses interest in the idea of beauty v. efficacy. Beauty v. efficacy is an important topic to touch on, with beauty being the phenomenal experience of the conscious mind, and efficacy being a form of action, specifically an object's power to do something. Other researchers tend to overstate consciousness’s role because they judge art through beauty rather than efficacy- a bias Gell deliberately avoids.",
        "Given that distinction, here's where we actually see it tested. The Malakulan ghost story involves sand-drawings of Malakula as a kind of trial by geometry- a maze the ghost must navigate correctly or be devoured. A ghost travels the road to the land of the dead; a guardian rubs out half of a completed figure drawn in the sand; the ghost must redraw the missing half correctly or be devoured. Despite being a work of art, the drawing represents far more than aesthetics- it was never about what role consciousness plays in it. Or in other words, it is not the question that he believes is being asked. The ghost can be right or wrong in this maze that it is partaking in, and it can either reconstruct the right pattern or not. Success and failure are procedural facts, verifiable from the outside (efficacy), and the account never asks what the ghost's redrawing of the pattern feels like from within (beauty). This means that he doesn’t explain this outcome with consciousness, but rather by redefining the agency that artwork can have. What Gell is attempting to prove is that consciousness does not trigger these responses, and our minds are rather referring back to prior experiences to formulate these secondary ideas.",
        "Furthermore, the phrase “outwit the demon” initially suggests that the ghost is consciously trying to outsmart the demon. But, Gell, instead, believes that it is easier to view this situation in terms of index and agency. Agency in this text is referred to as the capacity for something to cause an effect, while indexes are the visible signs of that particular agency. With these definitions in mind, Gell is explaining the ghost’s underlying capability of memory, trained motor skill, and cognitive processes as the primary agent. So, it is not the power of “consciousness” that led them to their success but rather the agency that already existed within them. In fact, Gell shares, “It is surely useful to consider the act of drawing as akin to dancing, and the design as a kind of frozen residue left by this manual ballet” (95). Gell's Melanesian material explains why pattern matters — protection, survival, reputation, kinship structure — using only publicly checkable performance, “like dances”. In simpler terms, the Malakulans theorize capability rather than consciousness. Whatever knowledge or skill that was used to allow the ghost to reconstruct the missing half, was already placed before he even began to redraw it. The drawing is not a product of new knowledge, it is rather an external record of prior competence. Consciousness, if present anywhere in this story, is not doing any of the work. The pattern that tests the true capability of the ghost is the mark left in the sand- the record of a maze successfully retraced, not a maze consciously understood. This shows that the passage does not argue for epiphenomenalism as a philosophical thesis- it simply proceeds as though the question never needed asking.",
        "If the ghost hesitated, or gave reason to believe that consciousness was involved in their action and thought process, there would be underlying doubt in Gell’s argument. However, all that was expressed was the drawing in the sand itself, not giving the reader access to any inner thoughts. The absence of that contemplation in the story, has Gell hypothesizing that the inner workings of our mind are a jumble of our own account of judgement, risk, and mortal consequence that cannot be categorized into the miniscule idea of one's consciousness."
      ]
