      "member": "robert",
      "n": 3,
      "title": "The Unfinished Story of the Mind",
      "summary": "Shifts from where an object’s agency comes from to what patterns do to the observer: they prompt narration, linking past difficulties to future expectations.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "In Build 1, I read Gell’s claim that a person is “not confined to particular spatio-temporal coordinates” (222) as a problem of identifying the source of agency: if agency extends through objects like the lime container, whose agency is operating? Rereading his discussion of decorative patterns shifts my question from where an object’s agency comes from to what it does to the observer. Patterns do more than carry a maker’s agency outward; they act on the viewer’s attention over time. This returns me to narration as a quality of consciousness, which is the coordination of successive experiences into a sequence organized around a purpose. What happens when an object invites the observer to begin such a sequence while continually postponing its completion?",
        "Gell shows how a pattern can begin this process. Decorated surfaces produce a “mazy dance in which our eyes become readily lost” (76), and he explains that the apparent movement originates in the observer’s perception rather than the object itself (78). The pattern is stationary, but the eyes keep moving between motifs, transforming separate visual impressions into apparent motion. This creates a succession of experiences, but succession alone does not establish narration. A wandering eye can follow a sequence without connecting its movements to a purpose. Gell’s description of “pleasurable frustration” (80) suggests what could supply that purpose. I interpret frustration as indicating an effort to understand that remains unsatisfied while pleasure gives the observer a reason to continue. Each new attempt can then respond to what an earlier attempt failed to show. The encounters become connected through the expectation that continuing trying to understand might produce understanding.",
        "This connection helps explain why Gell describes patterns as “unfinished business” (81). Their complexity creates relationships “over time between people and things” (81), extending the encounter beyond a single act of looking. The purpose of understanding gives separate encounters a direction, even when that purpose remains unfulfilled. Yet continued effort could also be described as perceptual problem-solving rather than narration. Calling this experience narration depends on more than the observer trying again. What they failed to understand during one encounter shapes what they look for in the next. Earlier experiences and future expectations become connected through the purpose of understanding the pattern.",
        "The Cretan maze tests both this connection and its limits. Gell observes that someone may know there is a way through a maze while still being unable to see that path without “laboriously” tracing its winding course (89). Knowing that an ending exists does not reveal the sequence needed to reach it. The observer must connect each turn to the larger purpose of finding a route, differentiating the anticipation of an ending from the process of reaching it. However, the maze also complicates my comparison. Its route can eventually be traced, whereas an intricate decorative pattern may resist harder to being traced. The maze therefore supports the distinction between knowing a solution exists and working toward it, but it does not prove that every pattern can be understood completely. For decorative patterns, the observer’s expectation of further understanding may be enough to sustain engagement. What connects successive encounters is the possibility that another attempt will disclose something new, not certainty that a final interpretation awaits.",
        "This returns me to the question I had in the beginning of whose agency operates, but changes what answering it requires. In Build 1, I focused on tracing agency back to its source. Now, I see that identifying a source does not fully explain how an object affects someone. Reading Gell through narration suggests a more specific process: patterns direct attention and encourage observers to connect past difficulties with future expectations. This helps explain why their effects can persist across encounters, sustaining relationships between people and things. Gell’s account, in turn, complicates narration by suggesting that a purposeful sequence can continue without reaching a destination. The observer makes connections among experiences, while the object’s complexity shapes the difficulties and possibilities around which those connections form. The question is, therefore, not only whose agency the object carries, but how the observer’s effort to understand becomes organized through a relationship with it."
      ]
