      "title": "A mazy dance",
      "summary": "Close-reads Gell’s claim that decoration is “animated”: patterns produce agency through the relations between motifs, and Gell’s own sentences move the reader the same way.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "> Passage: \"We are, indeed, accustomed to speak of decorated surfaces (as well as representational indexes with a lot of physical action in them) as 'animated'. This idiom reflects the fact that the motifs in decorative art often do seem to be engaged in a mazy dance in which our eyes become readily lost. We need a formula which captures the inherent agency in decorative forms, forms which do not simply refer to (represent) agency in the external world, but which produce agency in the physical body of the index itself, so that it becomes a 'living thing' without recourse to the imitation of any living thing. Decoration makes objects come alive in a non-representational way. The root of a pattern is the motif, which enters into relationships with neighbouring motifs, relations which animate the index as a whole. However, we can describe the relevant part-to-part and part-to-whole relationships in patterns much more precisely. Patterns can be distinguished from all other indexes by virtue of the fact that they have salient visual properties of repetitiveness and symmetry.\" (Gell, 76-77)",
        "Gell claims that referring to decorated surfaces as “animated” reflects how their motifs engage with people in an almost living way. By describing decoration as “animated,” Gell presents patterns as something more than passive visual features. He describes their motifs as participating in a “mazy dance in which our eyes become readily lost” (Gell, 76-77). His choice of “mazy dance” personifies the patterns by presenting them as things that can act and move, even though they do not possess their own will. Their apparent movement instead occurs through the viewer’s interaction with them. As the viewer follows the relationships between different motifs, the eye moves across the surface and can become “lost” within the pattern. The decoration therefore seems to act upon the viewer without literally being alive. This helps explain why Gell uses the language of animation: the pattern produces an experience similar to encountering something living through its ability to attract, direct, and affect the viewer.",
        "This idea leads to Gell’s larger argument that decorative forms possess a kind of agency of their own. He argues that these forms “do not simply refer to (represent) agency in the external world,” but instead “produce agency in the physical body of the index itself” (Gell, 76-77). The distinction between representing agency and producing agency is important because Gell does not believe that decoration needs to depict something living in order to create the impression of life. Instead, the decorative form can produce agency through its effect on the physical object and the person viewing it. This is why he argues that decoration makes objects “come alive in a non-representational way” (Gell, 76-77). The object does not become alive because it resembles a living thing; it becomes alive because its decoration creates an experience of activity and movement.",
        "Gell explains that the source of this effect is the relationship between individual motifs. He writes that “the root of a pattern is the motif,” which then “enters into relationships with neighbouring motifs” (Gell, 76-77). These relationships “animate the index as a whole” (Gell, 76-77). In other words, the agency of the decoration does not necessarily exist within any single motif. It emerges through the relationship between parts and the larger pattern. Gell emphasizes that patterns are characterized by “repetitiveness and symmetry” (Gell, 76-77), which cause the viewer to recognize connections and move between different parts of the design. The viewer’s eye becomes involved in the pattern rather than simply identifying what it represents. The visual structure itself therefore produces the sense of animation.",
        "The structure of Gell’s writing also reflects the experience he describes. His opening sentence about decorated surfaces being “animated” is long and clause-heavy, with a parenthetical interruption before reaching its main point (Gell, 76-77). The reader has to move through several parts of the sentence before arriving at the word “animated,” creating a small version of the visual movement Gell attributes to patterns. Like a decorative design, the sentence makes the reader move through different elements before understanding the whole. The syntax therefore performs the same kind of movement that Gell says causes “our eyes [to become] readily lost” in decoration (Gell, 76-77).",
        "Gell also repeatedly defines decoration negatively before providing a positive explanation. He says that decorative forms “do not simply refer to” agency but instead produce it, and that they become living things “without recourse to the imitation of any living thing” (Gell, 76-77). This delay mirrors his larger argument: he first rules out representation and imitation before explaining how decoration creates agency through its formal relationships. Finally, Gell’s avoidance of consciousness raises an important question about his understanding of agency. He never explains whether these objects possess consciousness or a will of their own, even though he describes them as agents capable of making objects seem alive. This could suggest that Gell does not believe consciousness is required for something to function as an agent. Alternatively, his understanding of consciousness and agency may be separate from the traditional assumption that agency is a product of conscious awareness. In either case, Gell’s argument challenges the idea that only conscious living beings can possess agency. For him, decoration can create agency through the physical and perceptual effects it produces, allowing an inanimate object to appear “alive” without representing life itself."
      ]
