      "title": "Becoming aware of looking",
      "summary": "Links Gell’s animated patterns to reflexivity: an object can have agency without consciousness, and encountering it can make a viewer aware of their own act of looking.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "In “Art and Agency,” Alfred Gell describes art as something that can have agency that affects the people who encounter it by attracting and distracting them. Gell's discussion of decorative patterns shows how an artwork can interrupt ordinary perception and make a person more aware of their own experience. His description of decorated surfaces as “animated” is especially relevant because he presents patterns as if capable of acting upon viewers even though they do not have their own consciousness or will. This idea can be connected to the quality of consciousness known as reflexivity, or the awareness that one is aware of something. Reflexivity differs from ordinary awareness because it involves becoming conscious of one's own process of thinking or perceiving. Through this interaction between the object and the viewer, Gell suggests that agency can exist without consciousness in the object, but that by means of that agency, the viewer's encounter with the object can create a more reflexive form of consciousness.",
        "Gell claims that referring to decorated surfaces as “animated” reflects how their motifs engage with people in an almost living way. He describes the motifs as participating in a “mazy dance in which our eyes become readily lost” (Gell, 76-77). The word “lost” evokes the image of an observer enchanted by a puzzling pattern, reminding the reader people may be captivated by these just as a fly by a bright light; however, interestingly, Gell himself expresses reflexivity, as he is aware of the effects of decoration on his perception – proving that by the means of reflection, the conscious being distincts itself from the un-conscious, as, unlike him, the fly may never understand and escape the lights captivation. On the other hand, “mazy dance” personifies the patterns by presenting them as things that can move and act, even though they do not actually have their own will. Instead, their apparent movement occurs through the viewer. A person looking at the pattern does not simply recognize the individual motifs. Their eyes move between them, follow their relationships, and can become lost in the larger design. This also creates an important connection to reflexivity because this way, the viewer can become aware not only of the pattern, but also of their own act of looking.",
        "This idea becomes more important when Gell explains that decorative forms do not need to represent something living in order to produce agency. He writes that decorative forms “do not simply refer to (represent) agency in the external world,” but instead “produce agency in the physical body of the index itself” (Gell, 76-77). This distinction separates agency from representation. Gell even states that “Decoration makes objects come alive in a non-representational way” (Gell, 76-77). He explains that “the root of a pattern is the motif,” which “enters into relationships with neighbouring motifs” (Gell, 76-77). These relationships “animate the index as a whole” (Gell, 76-77). Gell also identifies “repetitiveness and symmetry” as important visual properties of patterns (Gell, 76-77). These qualities direct the viewer's attention across the surface and cause the viewer to become involved in the process of looking. The person is therefore not simply observing the object but responding to its structure, which can make them more aware of their own perception.",
        "This also raises an important question about the relationship between consciousness and agency. Gell never suggests that the decorative object itself possesses consciousness or a will, even though he describes it as having agency. His avoidance of consciousness suggests that he does not believe consciousness is required for something to function as an agent. Gell's argument shows that an object can act upon a person's consciousness without being conscious itself. The viewer becomes aware of the pattern, but can also become aware of their own response to it. This is where it connects most clearly to reflexivity. The artwork does not simply give the viewer something to be aware of; it can make the viewer aware of their own awareness. Through its patterns, repetition, and apparent movement, the artwork interrupts ordinary perception and turns the viewer's attention back toward their own experience, proven by the author’s ability to identify this from his own experience. Gell's discussion therefore suggests that agency and consciousness do not have to exist in the same place, but that they are deeply related: the object can produce agency while the viewer’s consciousness is augmented by experiencing the reflexive awareness that results from encountering it."
      ]
